# Secrets (shh)

This project stores secrets in `.env.enc`. Commit that file. Do not commit a plaintext `.env` file.

## Commands

- `shh login` stores your key in the OS keyring.
- `shh set KEY -` reads a value from stdin.
- `shh rm KEY` removes one name.
- `shh list` shows names.
- `shh edit` opens the values in `$EDITOR`.
- `shh run -- CMD` starts a command with the values in the environment.
- `shh shell` starts a shell with the values.
- `shh env --stdout` prints export lines. Do not omit `--stdout`.
- `shh users add NAME` adds a recipient.
- `shh users remove NAME` rotates the data key.
- `shh doctor` checks the local setup. It does not check the MAC.

## Rules

- Do not log a secret value.
- Do not print a secret value.
- Do not pass a secret as a command argument. Use `shh set KEY -`.
- Do not edit `.env.enc` by hand.
- After `shh users remove` of an extractable key, change the secret values at the provider. Rotation does not change those values.
- Do not print `SHH_AGE_KEY`.
- `-e NAME` selects `NAME.env.enc`. The default file is `.env.enc`.
- If git reports a conflict in `.env.enc`, read `shh users list` before you commit. The merge can add or remove a recipient.
